  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday night through Sunday: A closed upper-level low will \ntrack across the Pacific Northwest Saturday night through Sunday\nmorning before shifting into western Montana and southern \nAlberta by Sunday afternoon. As the low center traverses \nnortheast Washington and northern Idaho, scattered rain showers \nwill spread from the Okanogan Highlands into northeast \nWashington and the Idaho Panhandle through Sunday morning. High \nconfidence (>80% probability) exists for rainfall totals \nexceeding 0.50 inches across the Okanogan Highlands. Amounts \ntrend lighter across northeast Washington and north Idaho, with \n10th to 90th percentile amounts ranging from 0.00-0.10 inches up\nto 0.25-0.70 inches, with the higher amounts focused along the \nCanadian border and higher terrain.\n\nMonday-Tuesday: Ensembles are in good agreement that an active \npattern will persist through the week, though a transient upper-\nlevel ridge will build into the region behind the exiting low \nearly week. This feature will promote warmer and drier \nconditions Monday and Tuesday, with daytime highs climbing into \nthe 80s. Winds remain light on Monday before becoming breezy on \nTuesday ahead of the next approaching weather system.\n\nTuesday night-Friday: Ensemble guidance is in excellent \nagreement regarding multiple weather systems impacting the \nPacific Northwest Tuesday night through Friday. The initial \nshortwave off the northern California and southwest Oregon coast\nwill lift northeast as a negatively tilted upper trough Tuesday\nnight into Wednesday, ahead of a secondary upper low digging \nfrom the Gulf of Alaska to offshore the Pacific Northwest. This \npattern brings widespread precipitation chances (40% or greater)\nand a 10-20% chance of thunderstorms Tuesday night and 10-15% \non Wednesday night. Good agreement continues into Thursday and \nFriday as the broader low tracks inland, with embedded \nshortwaves bringing multiple rounds of precipitation. Total \n72-hour 50th percentile precipitation from 5 AM Wednesday to 5 \nAM Saturday ranges from 0.15 to 0.40 inches from the lee of the \nCascades across the Columbia Basin into the Spokane Area and \nPalouse, 0.40 to 0.70 inches for the Idaho Panhandle, northeast \nWashington, and the Okanogan Highlands, and 0.50 to 1.00 inch \nalong the Cascade crest. /vmt",
